n on the University of Texas campus: Kallmann McKinnell & Wood's two limestone buildings (2006), the Michener Gallery Building with its skylight monitors and the smaller Edgar A. Smith Building, facing each other across the plaza with glazed atria and thin canopies; the plaza's 2023 petal shade canopies and live oaks; and Ellsworth Kelly's Austin (2018), a small white Latin cross of barrel vaults with coloured-glass windows. Footprints from the OpenStreetMap ground; description from general knowledge. No photographs were available: heights, the skylights, the canopies' number and shape, and the positions of Kelly's windows are approximate. Refined 6 Oct 2026 from published descriptions and the ground file's OpenStreetMap outlines, not photographs (none reachable): the Michener building stood about 18 m east of its outline and the Smith building was drawn twice as wide as its outline, so the Michener was moved 18 m west and 5 m south, the Smith narrowed to 18 m on its outline's west side, and the plaza between them (with its white canopies) and Kelly's Austin re-set in the gap; the street that ran under the museum moved to the east edge where the outlines show it, and the lawns and live oaks to the south and east added. On its outlines 62% before, 73% now. Still guessed: the heights, the roof monitors' number, Austin's exact place."}